WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.002 --> 00:00:07.446
"Parabiosis Experiments Prove 
Bloodborne Aging Factors"

00:00:07.446 --> 00:00:09.480
One of the major hallmarks
of agin

00:00:09.480 --> 00:00:13.020
is the decline of regenerative
capacity of our tissues.

00:00:13.020 --> 00:00:15.660
There are stem cells residing
in our muscles, for example,

00:00:15.660 --> 00:00:18.420
that can leap into service
at the first sign of injury

00:00:18.420 --> 00:00:19.560
to repair any damage.

00:00:19.560 --> 00:00:23.160
Is the waning
of tissue renewing abilities

00:00:23.160 --> 00:00:26.580
due to some intrinsic property
of aging stem cells

00:00:26.580 --> 00:00:30.300
or a consequence of being
trapped in an aging body?

00:00:30.300 --> 00:00:35.040
To find out, researchers grafted
the muscles of old rats

00:00:35.040 --> 00:00:37.440
into young rats and vice-versa.

00:00:37.440 --> 00:00:39.240
Inside the young rat,

00:00:39.240 --> 00:00:41.520
even the weak atrophied muscles

00:00:41.520 --> 00:00:43.980
of extremely old rats
at the end of their lives

00:00:43.980 --> 00:00:47.280
regained their strength, volume,
and ability to regenerate.

00:00:47.280 --> 00:00:48.780
They became young again,

00:00:48.780 --> 00:00:51.900
so the capacity
was still there all along.

00:00:51.900 --> 00:00:56.520
And young muscles in old rats
lost renewal capacity,

00:00:56.520 --> 00:01:00.660
so it appeared to be something
about the surrounding milieu

00:01:00.660 --> 00:01:03.300
rather than
inherent defects with age.

00:01:03.300 --> 00:01:05.880
To see if the critical elements

00:01:05.880 --> 00:01:07.560
were circulating
in the bloodstream,

00:01:07.560 --> 00:01:09.300
old muscle stem cells

00:01:09.300 --> 00:01:11.580
were cultured
in the blood of young animals.

00:01:11.580 --> 00:01:14.820
This alone had
a rejuvenating effect,

00:01:14.820 --> 00:01:18.300
suggesting that there may be
some sort of vitalizing factors

00:01:18.300 --> 00:01:21.720
in youth that we lose as we age,
or inversely,

00:01:21.720 --> 00:01:25.080
some repair-repressing component
that builds up.

00:01:25.080 --> 00:01:27.420
Either way,
this may be good news,

00:01:27.420 --> 00:01:30.180
because if we can find out
what those factors are,

00:01:30.180 --> 00:01:34.320
we may be able to slow aging
or even reverse it.

00:01:35.520 --> 00:01:38.340
Could more than just muscle
be restored?

00:01:38.340 --> 00:01:40.440
What about the brain
and all the other organs?

00:01:41.040 --> 00:01:44.460
To see the extent in which
circulating factors might play

00:01:44.460 --> 00:01:45.540
in affecting aging,

00:01:45.540 --> 00:01:49.860
researchers turned
to a macabre procedure

00:01:50.640 --> 00:01:54.720
called parabiosis, from the
Greek para, meaning "next to,"

00:01:54.720 --> 00:01:55.860
and bios, for "life."

00:01:56.460 --> 00:01:58.980
It was an attempt
to recreate the phenomenon

00:01:58.980 --> 00:02:01.140
of conjoined twins in a lab

00:02:01.140 --> 00:02:03.600
by sewing animals together

00:02:03.600 --> 00:02:06.840
to study the effects
of transmissible factors.

00:02:06.840 --> 00:02:11.040
Conjoined twins are often
referred to as "Siamese twins,"

00:02:11.040 --> 00:02:14.700
due to the notoriety of a 19th
century pair of Siamese-American

00:02:14.700 --> 00:02:16.200
brothers joined at the chest.

00:02:16.200 --> 00:02:20.940
The Blazek sisters are the
only conjoined twins on record

00:02:20.940 --> 00:02:22.920
ever having given birth.

00:02:22.920 --> 00:02:27.060
Josepha and Rosa Blazek
were quite literally

00:02:27.060 --> 00:02:28.140
joined at the hip.

00:02:28.140 --> 00:02:29.640
When Rosa got pregnant,

00:02:29.640 --> 00:02:33.240
both of their breasts developed
and started lactating,

00:02:33.240 --> 00:02:36.060
supporting the theory
we now know to be true today

00:02:36.060 --> 00:02:39.120
that lactation is
regulated by hormones

00:02:39.120 --> 00:02:40.260
that circulate in the blood.

00:02:40.920 --> 00:02:44.460
So what about aging factors
circulating in the blood?

00:02:44.460 --> 00:02:48.480
Surgically, researchers can
connect the skin, circulation,

00:02:48.480 --> 00:02:51.960
muscle walls, body cavities,
and shoulder blade joints

00:02:51.960 --> 00:02:52.800
of two animals.

00:02:53.340 --> 00:02:56.700
Early attempts to graft
different species failed--

00:02:56.700 --> 00:02:59.640
for example, mammals to birds
or a cat to a rat,

00:03:00.240 --> 00:03:04.320
but in 1862,
a pioneering French scientist

00:03:04.320 --> 00:03:09.600
was able to successfully pair
together two young rats.

00:03:10.800 --> 00:03:12.780
The first heterochronic union--

00:03:12.780 --> 00:03:15.480
hetero meaning "different,"
Chronos meaning "time"--

00:03:15.480 --> 00:03:19.320
the first heterochronic union
was created in 1955

00:03:19.320 --> 00:03:20.160
to answer the question:

00:03:20.160 --> 00:03:24.120
What would happen if you bathed
the tissues of an old animal

00:03:24.120 --> 00:03:25.740
in the blood of a young one?

00:03:26.460 --> 00:03:27.960
The title of the paper was,

00:03:27.960 --> 00:03:31.440
"Experimental Prolongation
of the Life Span."

00:03:32.460 --> 00:03:36.360
Old rats hooked up to young rats
lived about 20 percent longer

00:03:36.360 --> 00:03:38.100
than old rats
hooked up to one another.

00:03:39.060 --> 00:03:42.540
Subsequent experiments showed
old mice coupled with young

00:03:42.540 --> 00:03:45.180
became healthier, stronger,
and smarter.

00:03:45.180 --> 00:03:48.720
Aged tissues in a number
of organs were rejuvenated,

00:03:48.720 --> 00:03:52.020
including the brain, heart,
pancreas, skeleton, and muscles.

00:03:52.860 --> 00:03:56.700
That doesn't necessarily mean
that aging was slowed, though.

00:03:56.700 --> 00:04:00.240
Rather than some sort of
restorative bloodborne factor,

00:04:00.240 --> 00:04:02.520
maybe the older animals
were just taking advantage

00:04:02.520 --> 00:04:05.820
of the reserve organ capacity
in the younger animals,

00:04:05.820 --> 00:04:08.340
like having an extra set
of youthful kidneys.

00:04:08.340 --> 00:04:12.000
To see whether there's
some aging or anti-aging

00:04:12.000 --> 00:04:13.200
transmissible element,

00:04:13.200 --> 00:04:17.040
rather than sharing organs and
an entire circulatory system,

00:04:17.040 --> 00:04:20.760
what about just getting
a transfusion of young blood?

00:04:20.760 --> 00:04:22.140
After all,

00:04:22.140 --> 00:04:25.920
as an American Aging Association
journal review concluded:

00:04:25.920 --> 00:04:30.540
"The use of parabiosis in humans
is currently not performed

00:04:30.540 --> 00:04:32.280
due to the surgical
complications

00:04:32.280 --> 00:04:35.040
and resulting
undesirable lifestyle."

00:04:35.820 --> 00:04:37.320
Ya think?

00:04:37.320 --> 00:04:41.340
But getting a transfusion
of young blood would be easy.

00:04:41.340 --> 00:04:42.960
Does it work?

00:04:42.960 --> 00:04:45.720
We'll find out next.

